we can find the earlest such action with number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" (for each action, we can easily check if assumption \""},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is selected\" is contradicting with the action or not on linear time). Then, the answer is a number of electric shocks after action with number which is maximal among all such "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"-s."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"906B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/906/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"906B - Seating of Students"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The problem has many solutions, including random ones. Consider one of deterministic solutions. Without loss of generality, assume that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are a couple of corner cases:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1. In this case, good seating exists. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2. In this case, seating does not exist (obviously). "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 3. In any seating, one of neighbours of student 2 will be one of his former neighbours, so correct seating does not exist."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2. Only student 4 can be a neighbour of student 1, but there should be 2 neighbours for student 1; then, correct seating does not exist."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 3. Both students 5 and 2 have 3 neighbours in the initial seating; then, in new seating, these students should be in non-neighbouring corner cells. Moreover, these corner cells can not be in one row because in this case it's impossible to find a student for cell between 2 and 5. So, without loss of generality, let 5 be in lower left corner, and 2 — in upper right corner. Then, only students 1 and 3 can seat on lower middle cell; but if sduent 1 seats in the cell, then student 4 is impossible to seat at any of the remaining cells, so do student 6 in case of student 3 seating at the cell. So, correct seating does not exist in this case too."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 4. In this case, one of correct seatings is 2 4 1 3. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1;5 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". In this case, let students with odd numbers in increasing order will be in first half of the row, and others in increasing order - in second half. For example, for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 7 the seating will be 1 3 5 7 2 4 6."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 3. One of possible correct seatings is:"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"6 1 8"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7 5 3"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2 9 4;"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" If 2 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":";4 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then shift each even row cyclically on two symbols in the right, and then shift each even column cyclically on one symbol upwards. If students are vertical neighbours in the initial seating, then in new seating, they will be in different columns on the distance 2 (possibly through the edge of the table); but if students are horizontal neighbours in the initial seating, then in new seating they will be in neighbouring rows and neighbouring columns (possibly thorugh the edges again). So, for case 2 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", 4 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we build a correct seating."}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"906C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/906/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"906C - Party"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let’s formulate and prove several facts."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1. If we change an call order, the result doesn’t change. Let’s consider two vertices which are called consecutively. If they are not connected by edge, then regardless of the order, we get that at the end, neighbours of each vertex form a clique."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If they are connected, then independently on the order, we get clique from 2 vertices and all neighbours of them."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2. If graph is a tree, it’s sufficient to take as an answer all its vertices except leaves. Indeed, if we consider any 2 tree vertices, we get that all vertices on the way between them are in the answer. Each vertex reduces on 1 the distance between those 2, it means that the distance between them is 1."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3. Let’s select from source graph spanning tree, that has the largest number of leaves. One can say that we can use all vertices except leaves as an answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Obviously from point 2, that after all our operations with such set graph will become complete. Let’s show that it is minimal number of vertices."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let we selected some set of vertices, that is the answer. Then subgraph of given graph, built on the selected set of vertices, should be connected (otherwise between connected component can’t appear the edge and graph can’t be complete. Also, each of vertices, that isn’t in an answer should have at least one of neighbours selected (otherwise it is impossible to add new edge to it). Now let’s select a spanning tree in selected set (it’s possible because our set is connected) and add non-selected vertices into the tree as leafs. Then we see that our selected can be represented as spanning tree in the initial graph, in which all selected vertices are all non-leaf vertices and possibly, some leafs; but leafs can be obviously removed from the selected set by proved above. So, one of optimal answers can be described as set of non-leaf vertices of spanning tree with minimal possible number of non-leaves and, as a consequence, with maximal possible number of leaves, QED."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"4. Implementation. It is necessary to implement an algorithm that should work for 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" or faster or with worse asymptotic but with non-asymptotical optimization. One of possible solutions is following. Let contain any subset of vertices as a "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-bit mask; for example, mask of a subset containing vertices {"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"} will be equal to 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscrip